  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I've seen an uptick of Canny posts re: loud worlds + finding better methods of controlling volume across worlds because of how volatile that can be when world-hopping.\n\nThe technical solution could be to set a global loudness threshold (~like an audio poly limit~) in integrated LUFS so worlds can be enforced by an accumulated perceived loudness vs. momentary loudness to avoid affecting worlds that are generally quiet, but contain loud moments (like jump scares.) Enforcing this could be difficult, and would ultimately force creators to learn audio concepts just to publish a world...so....\n\nMy proposed relative solution would be to simply include an audio clip in the SDK that is playing at the average perceived loudness of conversation in VRChat. Preferably, a prefab with all of the audio components set to unity gain to avoid issues. The audio clip could be looping pink noise, or even an actual recorded voice sample of someone [like Tupper (☞ﾟヮﾟ)☞] reading something.\n\nHaving this gain reference clip would let world creators set up their world's audio around a simulated VRChat VOIP track. Most people know how loud something should be relative to someone speaking so this would pretty much create a de facto loudness standard for worlds without anyone having to learn anything new.\n \nMy testing (recording conversations in VRC with all other types of audio set to 0%) showed that the average loudness of general [non-trolling] conversation in VRChat is roughly -27 LUFS (long term).",
